    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Visualized data in the field of medicine has become so ubiquitous by 2024 that it is hard to imagine a time when this wasn’t so. While anatomical drawings and illustrations had been part of anatomy textbooks since Vesalius’ De Humani Corpori Fabrica was published in 1543, maps and tables started appearing in medical literature only towards the end of the eighteenth century. This thesis explores the factors behind the rise of visualization of information in the field of medicine in the United States. To do so, it studies the creation of medical maps and tables by an American physician, Valentine Seaman (1770-1817), who was a pioneer in the use of visualized data, and whose innovative work is mentioned only in passing in the history of medicine. Piecing together evidence from medical pamphlets, newspaper articles, municipal records, books on cartography, and archives, this thesis investigates what might have inspired Seaman to think spatially about disease and then translate his thoughts into maps and tables. In 1795, three years after Seaman began his practice, New York suffered an epidemic of yellow fever – an enigmatic disease that had not been seen in New York since 1742. Noticing a geographic clustering of patients in a filthy area, Seaman made two disease-spot maps in 1797, to illustrate the role of filth in yellow fever. Further, in 1801, he initiated smallpox vaccinations in New York City, and from the data he collected he created a detailed table that showed the efficacy and safety of the vaccine. The thesis argues that Seaman created spatial representations of the information he had gathered, not only to understand disease causation, but also to create a voice for public health measures – sanitation and vaccination. In addition, special circumstances – the influence of Enlightenment ideas, developments in cartography and printing, and the condition of the city itself at the turn of the eighteenth century – facilitated the development of spatial visualization of data in the field of medicine.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
